For each option, compute the attribute closure of its determinant and see whether every requested right-side attribute is reached:

```formula
{A, C}⁺ = {A, B, C, D}
A⁺ = {A, B}
B⁺ = {B}
{A, B}⁺ = {A, B}
where
{A, C}⁺, A⁺, B⁺, {A, B}⁺: attribute closures under F = {A → B, BC → D}
A, B, C, D: attributes
BC: the set {B, C}
→: functionally determines
{ }: a set
In words: with A and C you get B (from A) and then D (from B and C); A alone or B alone never gets D.
```

The first and third options follow from starting with A and C. A alone and B alone cannot reach D. The last option is reflexive.

The target follows exactly when its entire right side is reached.

1. B is never on a right side: in every key.
2. B alone is not a key, so try B plus one attribute:

```formula
B⁺ = {B}
{A, B}⁺ = {A, B, C, D}
{B, C}⁺ = {A, B, C, D}
{B, D}⁺ = {A, B, C, D}
{C, D}⁺ = {A, C, D}
where
B⁺, {A, B}⁺, {B, C}⁺, {B, D}⁺, {C, D}⁺: attribute closures under F = {AB → C, C → D, D → A}
A, B, C, D: the attributes of R
AB: the set {A, B}
→: functionally determines
{ }: a set
In words: AB uses AB → C then C → D; BC uses C → D then D → A; BD uses D → A then AB → C. Without B nothing reaches B, so CD fails.
```

3. All three are minimal, since B alone fails.
4. **3 keys**, and every attribute is prime, which will matter for 3NF.

Equivalence means each FD set implies every member of the other. Check whether each candidate preserves the original B-to-C rule and avoids adding an unsupported rule:

```formula
B⁺ with G = {B, C}
B⁺ with H = {B}
B⁺ with K = {B, C}
B⁺ with M = {B}
C⁺ with M = {C, B}
C⁺ with F = {C}
where
B⁺, C⁺: the attribute closures of B and of C under the named set
F: {A → B, B → C, A → C}
G, H, K, M: the four candidate sets in the options
A, B, C: attributes
→: functionally determines
≡: is equivalent to
{ }: a set
In words: H and M cannot derive B → C, and M also adds C → B, which F does not imply. G and K derive everything in F and nothing more.
```

G and K retain the B-to-C rule and are equivalent to F. H loses that rule; M loses it and adds an unsupported reverse rule from C to B.

G and K derive all three original dependencies, and each of their members follows from F.
